LESSONS = [
L("Chapter 1 — The Purchase Receipt, field by field", "14", "<p>Selling 5 taught that the Delivery Note is where the system's word meets the world, outbound. The <b>Purchase Receipt</b> is the same gate facing the other way — the document standing at the dock when goods ARRIVE, whose <b>submission moves stock IN and writes valuation</b>. Everything the firm will later sell, count, and cost enters through this document, and its craft decides whether the warehouse's truth starts true.</p><ul><li><b>Supplier and the PO link</b> — created FROM the Purchase Order (Chapter 2's discipline), inheriting supplier, items, rates, and target warehouses; the PO's fulfilment ledger counts down as this document submits.</li><li><b>Posting date and time</b> — when possession was actually taken. The stock ledger is a chronology inbound exactly as outbound: valuation, stock-as-at, and every dispute read this date as fact, and Chapter 9 convicts the batching habit that fictionalises it.</li><li><b>The items grid</b> — per row: item, the quantity in the PO's UOM with its conversion (Buying 2's arithmetic at the moment it becomes bins — 180 Carton-12 entering as 2,160 units), the rate travelling from the PO, and TWO quantity fields that selling's gate never needed: <b>Accepted Qty</b> into the <b>target warehouse</b>, and <b>Rejected Qty</b> into a <b>rejected warehouse</b> — the quality fork built into the document itself (Chapter 4 owns it).</li><li><b>Serial and batch sections</b> — for tracked items, identities are ENTERED here: this is where serials and batches are BORN into your system (Chapter 3 — arguably the most consequential data-entry moment in the entire trading cycle).</li><li><b>Supplier document references</b> — their delivery note number, waybill, container and seal numbers for imports: the fields that tie your receipt to their paper, worth thirty seconds at the gate and hours in any later dispute.</li></ul><p><b>What submission does — and its weight.</b> Accepted quantities enter their warehouses; rejected quantities enter quarantine; <b>valuation is written</b> — the received stock carries cost from the PO's rates (completed by landed charges where they apply, Chapter 5), and that valuation is what flows to cost-of-goods when selling ships it and what margin reports subtract forever. The receipt is thus not warehouse paperwork; it is the birth certificate of every unit's cost and identity. Draft, check against the physical count and the PO, then submit — because inbound, exactly as outbound, submission is the first irreversible moment, and unwinding it is a real operation (cancellation while untouched; a formal return, Chapter 6, once reality has moved on).</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — The receiving discipline", "13", "<p>The gate is the last CHEAP moment of truth in the inbound chain: after it, every error costs a document trail to unwind; before it, errors cost a recount. This chapter is the discipline that spends the cheap moment well.</p><p><b>Create from the PO — the spine's inbound landing.</b> The receipt is made FROM the Purchase Order: rows pre-fill with the <b>exact remaining quantities</b> (the remainder machinery you know — a second drop pre-fills at what drop one left, never at the original total, never from memory), rates travel untouched, target warehouses arrive as planned, and the PO's received-percentage advances itself. The from-scratch receipt is Procurement 5's Pattern 2 committed at the dock: real stock moved, ledger orphaned, the three-way match disarmed before it ever runs.</p><p><b>Count before sign — the physical half of the match.</b> The three-way match (Procurement 7 completes it) has three legs: what was ORDERED (the PO), what was RECEIVED (this document), what was INVOICED (theirs). This chapter's leg is the physical one, and its integrity is simple and absolute: <b>the receipt records what was actually counted, not what the paperwork says arrived</b>. The sequence: the truck's contents counted against the PO (not against the supplier's delivery note — theirs says what they claim they sent; the PO says what you ordered; the count says what came); discrepancies recorded AS the receipt's quantities (short is short, damaged is rejected — Chapter 4); and only then the supplier's paper signed, annotated with any variance. Signing clean and sorting it out later converts your evidence into their receipt.</p><p><b>Partial receipts.</b> Deliveries split — Emzor's two drops, the import that clears in stages. Each arrival is its own receipt from the PO at its actual quantities; the PO tracks the accumulating received-percentage; the remainder stays visibly open (Partially Received) until delivered or short-closed (Procurement 5's Close discipline). Nothing about partiality requires side-lists: the PO's arithmetic is the authority, drop after drop.</p><p><b>Receiving speaks the PO's units.</b> The UOM drill from Buying 2, at its moment: the receipt enters quantities in the PO row's unit, and the conversion counts the ledger — 47 cartons and 6 loose units is two honest rows, never 47.5, never re-keyed piece-counts into carton rows. The factor-of-twelve disease is born at this desk or nowhere.</p><p><b>Submission follows the count.</b> The document is completed AFTER the physical verification, recording what was gathered in — never submitted from the truck's manifest and corrected later, which is the ledger dictating fiction first and confessing after (Selling 5's conviction, verbatim, inbound). At a busy dock the discipline costs minutes; its absence costs stocktakes.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — Serial & batch identities: born at this gate", "14", "<p>Selling 5 taught the recall drill and the serial disciplines — reading identities OUT. This chapter is where those identities come FROM: every serial and every batch in your system is born on a Purchase Receipt, and the quality of that birth decides whether the outbound disciplines can exist at all.</p><p><b>Serials at receipt.</b> An item flagged Has Serial No demands, on the receipt row, the exact serial numbers arriving — keyed or scanned from the physical units. From this moment each unit has a biography the system can write: received on this document, from this supplier, at this cost, on this date; then its life through stock, delivery, warranty, and return. The craft point that decides everything downstream: <b>capture from the units, not from the supplier's packing list</b> — lists lie (repacks, substitutions, transcription), and a serial recorded that never physically arrived is a ghost that surfaces as Selling 5's impossible-return years later. Scan-driven capture where barcodes exist; careful physical keying where they do not; never bulk-pasted paperwork.</p><p><b>Batches at receipt — with the expiry.</b> A batched item's receipt records WHICH lot arrived and — the field that carries regulatory weight — its <b>expiry date</b>, read from the physical cartons. This entry is the foundation of everything Selling 5 promised: FEFO picking exists only if expiries were captured truthfully; the NAFDAC recall drill (which customers received batch B-204?) is answerable in minutes only because this desk recorded B-204 when it arrived; and shelf-life management (refusing goods arriving with unacceptably short dating — a standing receiving rule for pharma: minimum remaining shelf life per the firm's policy, goods under it rejected at the gate, Chapter 4's fork) begins with reading the carton. The full provenance chain now closes: supplier batch documentation (Procurement 1's onboarding gate) → batch captured at receipt → batch on every outbound DN → recall answerable in both directions: which customers got it, AND which supplier sent it.</p><p><b>The two-sided recall.</b> Walk it once, whole: the regulator names batch B-204. Outbound: DNs filtered by batch — customers, branches, quantities, dates (Selling 5's drill). Inbound: the receipt that brought B-204 names the supplier, the PO, the date, and the supplier's own batch paperwork filed against it — the conversation with the manufacturer and NAFDAC proceeds from documents on both sides. A firm whose gate captures identities runs this in minutes; a firm whose gate waves trucks through runs it in weeks, in the newspapers.</p><p><b>Cost of capture, priced honestly.</b> Identity capture is the slowest part of receiving, and clients push back. The consultant's answer is arithmetic: minutes per receipt against the recall that takes weeks, the warranty dispute that has no answer, the audit that cannot count. The premium is real; the coverage is the entire downstream edifice — and the flags were set at item creation (Buying 2) precisely so this desk has no discretion to skip it.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — Rejections, quarantine & quality inspection", "14", "<p>Not everything on the truck deserves your shelves. The receipt's quality fork — accepted vs rejected, each with its warehouse — is how the gate says NO with a paper trail, and this chapter is its full operation.</p><p><b>The fork's mechanics.</b> Each receipt row splits its counted quantity: <b>Accepted Qty</b> flows to the target warehouse as sellable (or usable) stock; <b>Rejected Qty</b> flows to the <b>rejected warehouse</b> — quarantine — where it is IN your possession but OUT of your sellable truth. Both movements happen at submission, both are valued, both are visible: the three damaged cartons exist, in quarantine, awaiting their fate, rather than existing nowhere (refused undocumented at the gate, then disputed from memory) or existing wrongly (waved into good stock to keep the queue moving — Chapter 9's conviction).</p><p><b>What gets rejected — the gate's standing rules, written.</b> Transit damage (crushed, wet, broken seals); wrong goods (the substitution Procurement 4 warned about, caught with the truck still present); short-dated stock (under the firm's minimum-remaining-shelf-life rule for expiring goods — a POLICY number, not a mood); failed inspection (below). The rules live on paper at the desk, because a gate that rejects by individual judgment rejects inconsistently, and inconsistency is what suppliers negotiate against.</p><p><b>Quality inspection — the formal layer.</b> Items flagged <b>Inspection Required (on Purchase Receipt)</b> demand a <b>Quality Inspection</b> before the receipt submits: a checklist document — parameters, readings, accept/reject verdict — per the item's template. The layer is proportionate by design: pharma actives, food lines, and safety-critical goods carry the flag; stationery does not. Where it applies, the inspection is the rejection's evidence: the verdict document, attached to the receipt, is what the supplier conversation and the NAFDAC file both cite. Inspections are honest only if performed — the pencil-whipped checklist (all boxes ticked at the desk without the goods opened) is worse than no layer, because it manufactures evidence of diligence that was not done.</p><p><b>The fate of quarantine.</b> Rejected stock resolves on a clock, not by sedimentation: <b>return to supplier</b> (Chapter 6 — the usual fate, with the debit note as its money half); <b>negotiated keep</b> (the supplier discounts the damaged lot and it moves — deliberately, documented — to sellable or salvage); or <b>write-off</b> (disposal, documented, with the cost landing where the accountant directs). The weekly quarantine review — what is here, why, whose next step — is the small ritual that keeps the rejected warehouse a processing bay instead of a museum: quarantine that only fills is a warehouse of undecided money.</p><p><b>The supplier conversation.</b> Every rejection is data with a name: rejection rates per supplier, by reason, per period — feeding the scorecard (Procurement 8) and the quarterly review. The supplier whose damage rate doubles has a packaging problem or a haulier problem; the one whose substitutions recur has a discipline problem; both conversations proceed from receipts, not recollections.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Landed cost: completing the truth of what things cost", "14", "<p>Procurement 2, Chapter 7 installed the THINKING — the invoice price is an ingredient, not the answer. This chapter installs the MECHANICS: how freight, duty, and clearing become part of received stock's valuation, so the numbers the firm trades on are the numbers reality charged.</p><p><b>The instrument: the Landed Cost Voucher.</b> After (or alongside) the receipt, the voucher names: the <b>purchase receipt(s)</b> it applies to; the <b>charges</b> being added — each with its amount and its own supporting document (the freight invoice, the duty assessment, the clearing agent's bill, the haulage receipt); and the <b>allocation basis</b> spreading those charges across the received items — by value (the default logic: costlier items bear proportionally more) or by quantity (where handling cost tracks units, not worth). Submission does the arithmetic: each received item's <b>valuation rises by its allocated share</b>, and the stock ledger now carries units at their true landed figure.</p><p><b>What changes downstream — the whole point.</b> Margin reports subtract the TRUE cost when selling ships these units; the import line's profitability is measured against reality instead of flattered by a factory price; Buying 2's viability pages reconcile against actuals (the estimated stack vs the voucher's actual stack — the variance is the import lane learning); and the accountant's P&amp;L stops eating clearing costs as anonymous expenses while gross margin lies. The alternative — charges expensed into general buckets — was convicted in Procurement 2, Chapter 9 and stands convicted here with the fix in hand.</p><p><b>Timing and estimates.</b> Charges arrive on their own schedules: freight billed at shipment, duty at clearing, the agent weeks later. The disciplines: voucher promptly per shipment as charges land (one shipment's stack in one or few vouchers, filed against its receipts — not a quarterly omnibus that no shipment can be reconciled from); and where goods must sell before the last bill arrives, the firm's policy on estimated charges (booked, then trued) is agreed WITH the accountant — the consultant's job is knowing the policy exists and encoding it, not inventing it per container.</p><p><b>The shipment file, completed.</b> Procurement 1 opened the import documentation habit; here it closes: the receipt, the voucher, and every charge's paper filed together per shipment. The payoffs, restated as operations: the true cost of container X is a lookup; clearing-cost drift across shipments is visible (the agent whose fees crept 30% in a year is a negotiation, found by comparing vouchers); and the FX question — what did this container really cost in naira, and what does the next one cost at today's rate — is answered from records.</p><p><b>Scope discipline.</b> Landed cost carries charges that attach to ACQUIRING the stock — freight, insurance, duty, clearing, inbound haulage. It does not become a dumping ground for general overheads, storage, or costs of running the warehouse; the accountant's line on what capitalises into stock is the line, and blurring it distorts valuation in the opposite direction from expensing. The instrument's power is precision; used loosely it just relocates the lie.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — Returns to supplier", "13", "<p>Goods go back — the rejected lot, the recall pulled inward, the over-supply agreed away. The purchase return is Selling 5's return machinery with the direction reversed, and its properties are the same three no shortcut has.</p><p><b>The instrument.</b> A purchase return is a <b>Purchase Receipt with Is Return ticked, made against the original receipt</b>, carrying <b>negative quantities</b>: submission moves stock OUT — from quarantine (the usual source: the rejected lot going home) or from good stock (the recall, the negotiated give-back) — written in the same ledger as everything else.</p><p><b>The three properties, inbound edition.</b> <b>Traceability</b>: the return references the precise original receipt — which delivery, which PO, which serials or batch going back; the supplier's credit conversation proceeds from a document chain, not a dispute. <b>Valuation</b>: the return exits stock at the value it entered at — including its landed share where a voucher applied — so the ledger's cost truth survives the round trip; a bare stock adjustment guesses, and guesses compound. <b>Identity</b>: serials return by name, batches by lot — the biography records the exit, and the two-sided recall trail (Chapter 3) stays whole: B-204 arrived on this receipt, went back on this return.</p><p><b>The money half — named now, owned next module.</b> The return receipt reverses STOCK; the supplier's ledger is corrected by the <b>debit note</b> — a return Purchase Invoice against the original — Procurement 7's Chapter. Hold the pairing as its sentence: <i>stock reverses on the return receipt; money reverses on the debit note; one physical event, two ledgers, two linked documents</i> — the exact mirror of the credit-note pairing you already hold from the selling side. What never substitutes: netting the return against the next delivery by handshake (two ledgers now disagree with one memory), or a supplier's verbal promise of credit (Chapter 9 meets what those promises age into).</p><p><b>Replacements.</b> The supplier replaces the damaged lot: the replacement arrives as a NEW receipt against the PO (or its own agreed flow) — two movements, two documents, the identity trail showing the swap explicitly. Never an edit to the return, never no-paper because it netted out — fourth appearance of the two-movements law, and its last needed.</p><p><b>Logistics of the give-back.</b> The physical return rides paper too: the supplier's collection reference or your outbound waybill recorded on the return document; quarantine's outflow reconciling to the return's quantities; and the weekly quarantine review (Chapter 4) confirming that what was resolved as return-to-supplier actually LEFT — the failure mode being goods written back to the supplier on paper while the pallets age in quarantine, which is a stock lie with a courtesy title.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — The gate rules: tolerance, shortage & the unordered truck", "13", "<p>Three standing rules govern what the gate accepts, and each is policy-in-configuration rather than dock-side improvisation. This chapter is the three, plus the posture behind them.</p><p><b>Rule one — over-receipt tolerance.</b> The mirror of Selling 4's over-delivery allowance: the receipt may exceed the PO's remaining quantity only within the item's <b>over-receipt allowance</b> (per item, global fallback — the Buying Settings number Procurement 8 owns). Within tolerance, the overage enters honestly and the row completes; beyond it, the receipt refuses to submit and the conversation happens with the truck present — accept formally (the PO amended or the policy's exception logged), or send the excess back on the spot. The policy logic transfers verbatim: <b>zero tolerance for serialized goods</b> (identity classes have no honest overage), a deliberate few percent for bulk (the 101st bag of cement blocks nobody), and never — the standing conviction — tolerance decided ad hoc at the dock.</p><p><b>Rule two — shortage is the PO's business, not the receipt's.</b> The truck brings 160 against a remaining 180: the receipt records 160 — full stop. The 20 stays where it belongs: OPEN on the PO as remaining quantity, visible in the To-Receive views, chased by the expediting craft (Procurement 5) or short-closed and resourced when the supplier concedes. What the receipt never does: record 180 because the paperwork promised it (fiction at the gate), or quietly absorb the shortage by editing history. Short is short, on the document, with the supplier's delivery note annotated to match — the variance evidence the credit conversation will need.</p><p><b>Rule three — the unordered truck.</b> Goods arrive that no PO ordered: the supplier's <i>while we are delivering, we added...</i>, the substitution beyond spec, the outright wrong consignment. The gate's answer is procedural, not personal: <b>no PO, no receipt</b> — unordered goods are not received into stock on dock-side goodwill, because a receipt without a commitment behind it has no rate, no match, no ancestry, and becomes an invoice dispute with your signature already on it. The paths that exist: refuse and send back (the default); or, where the addition is genuinely wanted, the commitment is created FIRST (a PO raised through whatever policy speed the situation merits — even an emergency PO is a document) and the receipt follows it. The rule's phrasing for the receiving team is one line: <i>we receive against orders, not against trucks.</i></p><p><b>The posture beneath all three.</b> The gate is where your firm's process meets other firms' improvisation, and the rules exist so the meeting is predictable: suppliers learn within weeks that overages need conversations, shortages get annotated, and unordered goods ride home — and their OWN dispatch discipline improves to match, because process at the gate is contagious in both directions. A gate that improvises trains the market to improvise at it.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Case study: three arrivals at HealthTrade", "16", "<p>Three trucks, every chapter working. The threads continue from Procurement 5. Each step names its chapter.</p><p><b>Arrival one — Emzor drop one: 180 cartons (Ch. 1-4, 6).</b> The receipt is created FROM the PO (pre-filled at the 300 remaining; edited to the drop's 180 — 2,160 units via the conversion). The count runs against the PO: 177 cartons sound, 3 crushed in transit. <b>Batch capture</b>: two lots on the truck — B-1147 (expiry 03/2028) and B-1152 (expiry 07/2028) — keyed from the physical cartons, quantities per lot; the remaining-shelf-life rule checks both against policy: pass. The receipt submits: <b>177 accepted</b> to Central, <b>3 rejected</b> to Quarantine, valuation written at the PO's ₦9,600. Emzor's delivery note is signed ANNOTATED: 180 counted, 3 damage-rejected. Within the week: the <b>return receipt</b> against this receipt — negative 3, batch-identified, quarantine outflow — with the debit note flagged to Procurement 7; Emzor's replacement 3 arrive days later as a NEW receipt. The PO ledger reads honestly throughout; the rejection enters Emzor's quality record (3/180 transit damage — a haulier conversation, not a crisis).</p><p><b>Arrival two — the chiller (Ch. 3, 4).</b> Vendor A delivers on the revised committed date. Inspection Required is flagged on the item: the <b>Quality Inspection</b> runs its checklist — model against spec (the 350L confirmed; Procurement 4's substitution warning honoured at the last line of defence), power-on test, condition — verdict Accept, document attached. The <b>serial</b> CHL-2418839 is keyed from the unit's plate: its biography opens — received from Vendor A, this PO, this date, ₦1.395m — the record every future service call will read. Accepted to the Facilities store; the launch proceeds with paper behind it.</p><p><b>Arrival three — the import container (Ch. 2, 5).</b> The Mumbai consignment clears in two stages; each stage is its own receipt from the PO at actual counted quantities, container and seal numbers recorded, batches and expiries keyed per lot. Then the <b>Landed Cost Voucher</b>: ocean freight (invoice attached), duty (assessment attached), clearing and terminal charges (agent's bill), haulage — allocated by value across the container's items. Valuations rise to true landed figures; the viability page's estimated stack reconciles against the voucher's actual (variance: clearing ran ₦180k over estimate — noted, and the agent's next bill will be compared). The shipment file closes complete: PO, receipts, voucher, every charge's paper — the container's true naira cost a permanent one-minute lookup.</p><p><b>The week's quarantine review (Ch. 4).</b> One line only — Emzor's 3, already resolved as returned-and-replaced, outflow confirmed. The bay is a processing area, not a museum; the gate ends the week the way it started it: honest.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common mistakes & support patterns", "13", "<p>The receiving gate's scar tissue — and inbound, the diseases are the ones that poison VALUATION and PROVENANCE, the two truths hardest to rebuild. Symptom → disease → fix.</p><p><b>Pattern 1 — \"The receipt was submitted from the manifest; the count came later.\"</b> Disease: fiction first, confession later (Ch. 2) — the ledger dictated by paperwork, corrected by embarrassment. Fix: submission follows the count, structurally — the document completed at the dock after verification; the manifest is the supplier's claim, not your record.</p><p><b>Pattern 2 — \"The recall query returns nothing — but we definitely stocked that batch.\"</b> Disease: batch capture skipped or keyed wrong at receipt (Ch. 3) — the identity was never born, so no downstream discipline can find it. Fix: reconstruct the affected window painfully once (supplier documentation against sales records); then capture-from-the-cartons as an audited gate rule — the recall that cannot run is the one that reaches the newspapers.</p><p><b>Pattern 3 — \"Damaged goods are in sellable stock.\"</b> Disease: the fork unused — rejects waved into good stock to keep the queue moving (Ch. 4). The damage surfaces at the customer, which is the most expensive place to discover it, with a return and a credit note now owed OUTBOUND. Fix: the rejected-warehouse fork as routine, the standing rejection rules at the desk, and the weekly quarantine review that proves the fork is being used.</p><p><b>Pattern 4 — \"The import line looks profitable; the P&amp;L disagrees.\"</b> Disease: landed charges expensed while stock sits undervalued (Ch. 5) — Procurement 2's warning, now with the fix installed and skipped. Fix: the Landed Cost Voucher per shipment, charges papered and allocated; the viability pages reconciled against actuals.</p><p><b>Pattern 5 — \"The supplier promised credit for the returns... in March.\"</b> Disease: verbal-credit aging (Ch. 6) — stock went back on a handshake; no return receipt, no debit note, and the supplier's statement now disagrees with your memory. Fix: the pairing enforced — return receipt for the stock, debit note for the money (Procurement 7) — and the statement reconciliation (Procurement 1) that would have caught it at one month old.</p><p><b>Pattern 6 — \"We accepted the extra pallets — they were already off the truck.\"</b> Disease: the unordered truck received on goodwill (Ch. 7) — no rate, no match, no ancestry, and the invoice dispute arrives with your signature on the delivery note. Fix: we receive against orders, not against trucks — refuse, or paper the commitment first; the gate rules posted at the desk.</p><p><b>Pattern 7 — \"Friday's arrivals were all posted Monday.\"</b> Disease: posting-date batching (Ch. 1) — the chronology fictionalised for convenience; stock-as-at, valuation timing, and dispute evidence all read a weekend that never happened. Fix: post at possession; the governance version of date adjustments belongs to accounting policy, not dock convenience — the same law as the outbound gate, because it is the same ledger.</p><p><b>The law.</b> Procurement 1: purchase documents are downstream of supplier masters. Procurement 2: cost truth begins at the item. Procurement 3: the request layer separates need from commitment. Procurement 4: sourcing is a decision record. Procurement 5: the PO is the single source of inbound truth. This module adds the sixth: <b>the receiving gate is where the world enters the system — keep it honest, and every valuation, every recall, every match downstream can be believed.</b> Count before signing, capture identities from the goods, fork the damaged, land the true cost, return with paper, and receive against orders. A firm whose gate holds these disciplines trusts its own shelves; a firm whose gate improvises audits them forever.</p>"),
]

